 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** On outdoor datasets, AligNeRF achieves a PSNR more than 3 points higher than standard NeRF and better than the best mip-NeRF results [S2].
-*   **Efficiency:** The method does not significantly increase training time compared to the baseline [S2].
-*   **Scalability:** The convolution-augmented architecture reduces the number of parameters compared to purely dense MLPs for high-resolution settings [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** The method is designed as a plug-in for NeRF-like models (point-sampling and frustum-based) but relies on the assumption that misalignment is the primary source of error in high-frequency details [S1].
+*   **Performance:** On outdoor datasets, AligNeRF achieves a PSNR more than 3 points higher than standard NeRF and better than the best mip-NeRF result [S2].
+*   **Training Time:** Does not significantly increase training time compared to the baseline [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves high-frequency detail recovery and handles misalignment from moving objects or calibration errors [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires patch-based sampling and additional CNN processing, increasing implementation complexity compared to standard NeRF [S1].
 
 ## Background
-AligNeRF was introduced by Yifan Jiang, Peter Hedman, Ben Mildenhall, Dejia Xu, Jonathan T. Barron, Zhangyang Wang, and Tianfan Xue [S1]. It was presented at CVPR 2023, building upon the mip-NeRF 360 baseline [S1]. The method addresses the limitations of training NeRF on high-resolution images, specifically focusing on moving objects and calibration errors [S2].
+AligNeRF was introduced by Yifan Jiang, Peter Hedman, Ben Mildenhall, Dejia Xu, Jonathan T. Barron, Zhangyang Wang, and Tianfan Xue in 2022 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of training NeRF on high-resolution images, specifically the degradation caused by moving objects and the inability of standard MLPs to capture local neighborhood information [S2]. The method is designed as an easy-to-plug-in component for any NeRF-like models, including point-sampling and frustum-based approaches [S1].
 
